WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.374
[VIOLIN MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:15.010
Well, MIT is a very
different kind of institute.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:20.260
Instead of becoming the
recipients of building industry

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:23.080
culture, we can now
change building industry

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.100
by the kinds of experiments
we're doing in school.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.730
MIT has a variety
of departments that

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:35.050
are so powerful that they have
the potential of radicalizing

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:38.500
what we do in design and in
the architecture department.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.660
Even the way that we've
structured our curriculum

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:44.830
around workshops and other
kinds of collaborations

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:50.140
foster and encourage this kind
of cross disciplinary platform.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:55.450
So you're situated
perfectly for the kinds

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.400
of things that only MIT can do.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.837
[CHIME]

00:01:00.753 --> 00:01:03.148
[BIRDS CHIRPING]

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:10.020
The invention of human
artifacts of any kind

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.860
always consists of three
elements, the geometry,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:19.602
the process, and the material
that constitute that artifact.

00:01:19.602 --> 00:01:21.084
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:27.260
It's very clear that new
materials engender new forms,

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:30.770
and architectural
design benefits

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:37.160
from addressing, applying new
materials in an informed way.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.220
The Emergent Materials Group
and the Urban Metabolism Group

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:44.090
share a central
interest in materials

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.460
for the built environment.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.760
Lately, we've been working a
great deal in the area of foam

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.410
to materials, especially
foam cements and concretes.

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.930
So typically a
beam in bending is

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:59.480
going to have the greatest
amount of stresses

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:00.830
on the top and bottom.

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:04.040
So we can reduce the
amount of material

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.880
on the interior of a beam.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.039
The work really tries
to address that there's

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:14.090
two main paradigms throughout
almost every industry.

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:16.310
One paradigm is
what can you design.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.140
What can you compute or analyze?

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:21.860
And the other paradigm is what
can you physically construct,

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:23.720
or what can you build.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.340
The work really tries to
traverse all scale lengths.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.280
It tries to work from
the nanoscale, which

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.370
is traditionally where we see
self-assembly and biological

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:36.710
examples, chemical examples.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.050
And it tries to
bridge that and say,

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.540
can't we use this
phenomenon of self-assembly

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:45.059
as a new paradigm for building
things at large scale.

00:02:45.059 --> 00:02:50.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:54.760
We work with any type of
material, synthetic or natural

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:58.630
materials, and really try to
design specific geometries that

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:00.425
have materials in mind.

00:03:00.425 --> 00:03:02.050
And when you design
those two together,

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:04.480
they can respond to energy,
and the energy sources

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.490
can be heat, shaking, gravity,
pneumatics, electronics.

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:11.425
So you can use any
type of energy,

00:03:11.425 --> 00:03:15.040
and when you mix that with
the geometry and material

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.570
properties, then
they can respond

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:18.910
and they can change state.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.640
Primarily, it will
first be applied

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.680
in extreme
environments-- scenarios

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.240
where it's difficult to build.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.016
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.930
The research and the
practice is really

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.150
about cross-disciplinary
collaborations

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.720
between designers,
scientists, engineers,

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.450
working on this phenomenon
of self-assembly,

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.490
discovering where we
find it naturally,

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.590
where we can use
it synthetically,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:47.396
and trying to apply that at
all different scale lengths.

00:03:47.396 --> 00:03:49.786
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.430
The focus of our work, at
least at the smaller scale,

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:06.870
at the scale of say art
installations or retail,

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.430
we leverage CNC
fabrication methods,

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:13.870
but not as a way to exude
their prowess necessarily,

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:18.011
but more as a way to help us get
at a particular formal agenda.

00:04:18.011 --> 00:04:20.471
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:28.640
We have an installation
called Totems which in a way

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.190
is a kind of
proto-architecture--

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:34.670
not quite architectural,
not quite sculptural,

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:38.150
but I think that it
offers a proposition

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:41.480
to each of those disciplines.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.370
We do a lot of
testing via 3D prints.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:47.210
A lot of the formal
investigations for Totems,

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:50.940
specifically,
happened in 3D prints.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:55.080
We did maybe 30 to 50 3D
prints of the Totems as a way

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.050
to understand how light
and shadows were working,

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:02.130
and how best the
anamorphic effects were

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:06.575
working before we went to
the large format wire cutter.

00:05:06.575 --> 00:05:08.515
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:15.890
In terms of collaborations, some
of those happen here at MIT.

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:20.180
Joel Lamere and I are
developing an installation now

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:23.900
which is an anechoic chamber.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:27.500
And the anechoic chamber
uses industrial felt,

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:30.478
both on its exterior and
as a way to absorb sound

00:05:30.478 --> 00:05:31.145
on the interior.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.540
I'm bringing a kind
of disciplinary,

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:38.940
or formal, or geometric
interest on the one hand,

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:42.330
also a certain set of
specialized understandings

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:45.780
of the behavior of the sheet
materials on the other.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:49.290
So my architectural
interests are really broad,

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.750
but in terms of my current
research preoccupation

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.450
it's about material
geometry, specifically

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:57.390
sheet shapes or sheet
material geometries.

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:01.808
How they bend,
stretch, stack, fold.

00:06:01.808 --> 00:06:03.600
But obviously understanding
sheet materials

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.120
is really critical
to architecture.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.970
This is sort of a long standing
discourse on sheet materials

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.460
and architecture in
part because it's

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.650
so ubiquitous in
conventional construction

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:17.550
so everything, including
plywood, sheet metal, steel,

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:20.820
aluminum, plastics,
all of these have

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:23.220
to deal with the developable
surface geometries which is

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.360
to say sheet shapes generally.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.010
And so expanding the total set
of understandings about sheet

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:31.050
shapes is a totally,
and kind of obviously,

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.105
worthwhile architectural
undertaking.

00:06:33.105 --> 00:06:35.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:40.260
These installations
attempt to jump

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.090
from the scale of
the paper model

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:45.030
to the scale of the
architectural space at the very

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:46.350
least.

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:58.590
Well, over the past 20
years, first as Office dA now

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:05.230
as NADAAA, we've identified
two or three areas of focus.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:11.250
The first has been to look at
materials and their behavior

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:14.250
as a point of departure for
many of the architectural

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:16.380
experiments we do.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:20.970
The second area is to
recognize that materials

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:23.910
don't have an endless limit.

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.460
Everything comes in
modules, in sheets,

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:31.920
in blocks, in bricks, and that
without aggregation, there's

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:36.720
no way of beginning to figure
out how construction works

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:37.340
in general.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:41.010
So the detail of
bringing things together,

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:46.500
as a point of departure, has
been another central focus.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.850
So this has been a
point of revolution

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:54.720
for a range of projects
that we've researched

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.800
and undertaken,
some of which are

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.890
at the scale of a
piece of furniture,

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:01.260
others that are at the
scale of a building,

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:04.836
and others that are really
at the scale of urbanism.

00:08:04.836 --> 00:08:07.166
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:12.050
One of the agendas
of the studio is

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:16.070
to create interactive
architectures for public space

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:19.640
through design and technology.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:22.100
And these projects
can range and scale

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:26.600
from the scale of the body,
such as the Defensible Dress

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:29.990
Project, which is a
wearable architecture which

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:32.539
uses a microcontroller
and shape memory

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:37.820
alloys to defend
your personal space,

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:41.250
to very public scale
infrastructure projects,

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:45.260
such as our proposal for the
Audi Urban Futures Initiative

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:49.460
where we're speculating on high
speed regional infrastructure

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:53.090
and transformations in
the city around issues

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:57.140
of mobility for the year 2030.

00:08:57.140 --> 00:09:00.740
It seems now more
and more necessary

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:04.580
that our environments have
to become smarter, more

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:07.820
responsive, and more flexible.

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:10.190
The way in which
architecture is made

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:12.560
needs to be completely
rethought if we're

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:17.030
to go beyond bricks and mortar
to engage the challenges

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:18.588
of the next century.

00:09:18.588 --> 00:09:20.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:29.820
My work as an architect
focuses on designing

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:33.810
architecture and urban
scale interventions that

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:37.290
engage the emergent
infrastructure of our time.

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:41.910
We're interested in how
design engages that mutability

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:44.310
between energy and matter--

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:47.460
when materials generate
energy or store it,

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.610
when materials are
dynamic and change state,

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:54.960
exchange heat, produce light,
or communicate information.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.720
The challenge, I think, for
the discipline of architecture

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:00.810
today isn't in
digital fabrication,

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:03.270
it's in the material
fabrication.

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:06.690
In thinking about what it
means to be material today,

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:10.440
when we move fluidly between
digital and physical realms,

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:12.990
how we use materials
and not waste them,

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:17.860
and how we form materials,
and how we transport them.

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.890
If we're going to change
the building industry

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:23.980
and regenerate an
innovation-based manufacturing

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:26.560
segment in the
United States, then I

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:29.050
think we need to do this
from within practice,

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:32.290
from working with the norms
of the building industry,

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:34.450
but not settling
for those norms.

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:38.710
Instead innovating to
both accept and transform

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:40.922
those standard conditions.

00:10:40.922 --> 00:10:43.282
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:50.330
I think we're pursuing
several initiatives relating

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:57.470
with computation material
at several different areas.

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:00.800
Dynamic interactivity
is one type of surface

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:02.420
and we're driving that forward.

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:07.490
Imagining actively alloplastic
environments, if you like,

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:10.460
it's a demonstration
of the power

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:13.400
of computation come real time.

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:16.070
Then in the robotic
manufacturing work,

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:24.030
we're looking for power
information and material

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:29.300
all coming together with control
directly at the architect's

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:30.570
workplace.

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:32.660
So we're developing tools
that allow architects

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:36.260
within their native
software, like Rhino to pilot

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:38.190
a powerful production
machine like a KUKA

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:41.090
robot demonstrating,
I think, what

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:44.570
will be the future of
manufacturing which

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:49.640
is sophisticated algorithms
and modeling that link directly

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:52.550
to sophisticated
manufacturing machines

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:58.017
and allow both new efficiencies
but new versatilies, new form

00:11:58.017 --> 00:11:59.790
of complexities.

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:02.150
I'm also frustrated by the
slowness of the building

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:04.220
industry to innovate materially.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:08.750
There's a fantastic amount of
research in material science

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:12.860
that's pent up and it's
just dying to come.

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:16.670
At a period where the
environmental concerns are

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:20.000
becoming so enormous and
the challenge of building

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:25.760
the developing world is so
extraordinary and necessary.

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.730
CAD Cam meets material
science, like composites,

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:32.850
I think is going to
breathe a whole new life

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:36.440
into the architectural
and building industries.

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:39.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

